ue greatest element m. Therefore, cases indexed by m are disjoint and cover F.","statement":"Partition F by the greatest element m of a subset."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["maximum-partition"],"id":"case-eight-seven","justification":"For m=8 the remainder must be 1, forcing {8,1}; for m=7 it must be 2, forcing {7,2}.","statement":"The m=8 and m=7 cases contribute one subset each."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["maximum-partition"],"id":"case-six","justification":"With m=6, smaller distinct positive elements must sum to 3. The only such subsets are {3} and {2,1}.","statement":"The m=6 case contributes {6,3} and {6,2,1}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["maximum-partition"],"id":"case-five","justification":"With m=5, smaller distinct positive elements must sum to 4. The only such subsets are {4} and {3,1}.","statement":"The m=5 case contributes {5,4} and {5,3,1}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["maximum-partition"],"id":"case-four","justification":"With m=4, the smaller elements are drawn from {1,2,3} and must sum to 5. Only {3,2} does so.","statement":"The m=4 case contributes {4,3,2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["maximum-partition"],"id":"small-maximum","justification":"If m≤3, all elements lie in {1,2,3}, whose total is 6<9.","statement":"No member of F has greatest element at most 3."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["case-eight-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","small-maximum"],"id":"total-count","justification":"The exhaustive disjoint cases contribute 1+1+2+2+1+0=7 subsets.","statement":"Summing the case counts gives |F|=7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
